Show the student an interactive step-by-step explanation: a diagram that stays in place and a panel that walks through one change per step. Use it when a mechanism, process or decomposition is easier to follow as a sequence than as text, and at most once per reply. Still answer in text: one or two sentences that say what the explanation shows.

The input is one complete explainer/1 content object with format "explainer/1", locale "de" or "en" (the reply language) and a built-in diagram in content.diagram:
- causal: persistent nodes and arrows with contrasting scenarios, e.g. how a policy rate change reaches inflation. At most 9 nodes.
- accounts: balance sheets that change step by step and stay balanced, e.g. a bank loan or a repo. At most 2 entities and 4 rows per side.
- parts: components that add up to a total, optionally per year, e.g. interest on savings.

Keep it small: at most 6 steps per track, 10 glossary entries and 4 presets. Words per item: lead 35, overview text 35, path description 18, step title 10, step text 70. Write each step as: what changes, why, and what to notice. Mark illustrative numbers as illustrative and never invent sources.

Citations: the explanation cannot link to source cards, so never write [n] or [n, p. x] markers inside the content object. Put the inline [n] markers in your reply text as usual, and list the course documents the explanation draws on in footer.sources, each with its title and the page or section as locator.

Each parameter needs label, min, max (above min), step, value and format. In a parts diagram, every part id is also a key in components. Compute numbers with expressions in calc and values, not with other tools; the explanation recalculates them as the student moves a slider.

Text is plain: {term|label} opens a glossary entry with that id, \{{name}} inserts a value defined in values, and HTML is not allowed. Numbers come from expressions over params and calc, never from code.

Minimal valid objects to copy the shape from; a real explanation has more steps. A parts diagram:
{"format":"explainer/1","id":"simple-interest","version":1,"locale":"en","title":"Principal and interest after n years","lead":"Split a savings balance into the amount deposited and the interest earned. Values are illustrative.","params":{"order":["r","n"],"items":{"r":{"label":"Interest rate","min":0,"max":10,"step":0.5,"value":3,"format":{"style":"number","decimals":1,"unit":{"one":"%","other":"%"}}},"n":{"label":"Years","min":1,"max":10,"step":1,"value":5,"format":{"style":"number","unit":{"one":"year","other":"years"}}}}},"calc":{"p":"1000","interest":"round(p * ((1 + r / 100) ^ n - 1))"},"values":{"interest":{"expr":"interest","format":{"style":"money","currency":"CHF","decimals":0}}},"diagram":{"type":"parts","format":{"style":"money","currency":"CHF","decimals":0},"parts":{"principal":{"expr":"p"},"interest":{"expr":"interest"}},"total":{"expr":"p + interest","label":"Balance"}},"components":{"principal":{"label":"Principal","color":"grey"},"interest":{"label":"Interest","color":"green"}},"tracks":{"main":{"steps":["base","grow"]}},"steps":{"base":{"title":"Start with the deposit","text":"You deposit CHF 1,000. This {principal|principal} is the base of the balance.","data":{"show":["principal"]}},"grow":{"title":"Interest adds on top","text":"After the chosen years, interest adds \{{interest}}. Move the sliders to see how rate and time change it.","data":{"show":["interest"]}}},"glossary":{"principal":{"term":"Principal","definition":"The amount deposited at the start."}},"footer":{"assumptions":"Interest is credited yearly and left in the account. Illustrative rate."}}

A causal diagram: place every node on both grids as [column, row] inside that grid's size, and give paths a label, an overviewLabel and every path a name, short name, color and description:
{"format":"explainer/1","id":"oil-to-cpi","version":1,"locale":"en","title":"How an oil price rise reaches consumer prices","lead":"Follow one cause and its effects. The steps are qualitative.","diagram":{"type":"causal","grid":{"wide":{"cols":3,"rows":1},"tall":{"cols":1,"rows":3}},"nodes":{"oil":{"label":"Crude oil price","wide":[0,0],"tall":[0,0]},"fuel":{"label":"Fuel prices","wide":[1,0],"tall":[0,1]},"cpi":{"label":"Consumer prices","wide":[2,0],"tall":[0,2]}},"edges":{"oil_fuel":{"from":"oil","to":"fuel","path":"direct"},"fuel_cpi":{"from":"fuel","to":"cpi","path":"direct"}}},"paths":{"label":"Channel","overviewLabel":"Overview","order":["direct"],"items":{"direct":{"name":"Direct channel","short":"Direct","color":"orange","description":"Fuel prices follow crude within weeks."}}},"tracks":{"direct":{"path":"direct","steps":["impulse","fuel","cpi"]}},"steps":{"impulse":{"title":"The oil price jumps","text":"World crude prices rise after a supply disruption.","data":{"nodes":{"oil":"↑"},"edges":[]}},"fuel":{"title":"Fuel becomes more expensive","text":"Petrol and heating oil are made from crude, so their prices follow.","data":{"nodes":{"fuel":"↑"},"edges":["oil_fuel"]}},"cpi":{"title":"Measured inflation rises","text":"Fuel is part of the consumer basket, so the price index rises.","data":{"nodes":{"cpi":"↑"},"edges":["fuel_cpi"]}}},"footer":{"assumptions":"Qualitative sketch with one channel only."}}

An accounts diagram: each step sets the changed rows, and both sides of every balance sheet stay equal:
{"format":"explainer/1","id":"loan-deposit","version":1,"locale":"en","title":"How a bank loan creates a deposit","lead":"Follow one loan through the bank's and the borrower's balance sheets. Amounts are illustrative changes.","params":{"order":["amount"],"items":{"amount":{"label":"Loan amount","min":10000,"max":100000,"step":10000,"value":50000,"format":{"style":"money","currency":"CHF","decimals":0}}}},"values":{"amount":{"expr":"amount","format":{"style":"money","currency":"CHF","decimals":0}}},"diagram":{"type":"accounts","format":{"style":"money","currency":"CHF","decimals":0,"sign":"always"},"entities":{"bank":{"label":"Bank","assets":{"loans":"Loans"},"liabilities":{"deposits":"Deposits"}},"firm":{"label":"Borrower","assets":{"deposit":"Deposit"},"liabilities":{"loan":"Bank loan"}}}},"tracks":{"main":{"steps":["start","pay"]}},"steps":{"start":{"title":"Starting position","text":"Both balance sheets are shown as changes, so everything starts at zero."},"pay":{"title":"The bank pays out the loan","text":"The bank records a loan of \{{amount}} and credits the same amount as a new deposit. The borrower holds the deposit and owes the loan.","data":{"set":{"bank":{"loans":"amount","deposits":"amount"},"firm":{"deposit":"amount","loan":"amount"}},"flows":[{"from":"bank","to":"firm","label":"New deposit \{{amount}}","kind":"money"},{"from":"firm","to":"bank","label":"Promise to repay","kind":"claim"}]}}},"footer":{"assumptions":"One bank; the borrower keeps the deposit there."}}

If the tool reports errors, fix every listed error and call it again with the complete corrected object. After a third failed attempt, explain in text instead.
